IMPORT prelude

-- Biosecurity Act 2015 (Commonwealth) -- coming home through the airport: must I fill in the biosecurity part of the
-- incoming passenger declaration, may my goods come in, what happens to goods that should not have, and what can I be made
-- to pay for not declaring them or for a false answer?
--
-- SEAT: the incoming traveller (an individual entering Australian territory on an incoming aircraft or vessel, with
-- goods). Every decision is phrased as that traveller asks it, from facts they could state. Where the Act speaks to the
-- Director of Biosecurity, a biosecurity officer or a court (ss 126, 131-136, 173-174, 182, 196, 523-524, 628), the
-- encoding says what it means for the traveller: what they must give, what may be done to their goods, what they may be
-- asked to pay.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 2 (commencement, item 2); s 119 (goods brought in are subject to biosecurity control); s 126
-- (questions about goods); ss 131, 136 (biosecurity measures; destruction); s 162(1) (release from biosecurity control);
-- s 173 (prohibited goods); s 174 (conditionally non-prohibited goods); s 182(1), (2), (7) (suspended goods); s 185(1)-(3)
-- (bringing in prohibited or suspended goods); s 186(1)-(3), (7) (conditions not complied with); s 186A (concealment);
-- s 196(1)-(5) (persons on incoming aircraft: information and travel documents -- the incoming passenger declaration);
-- s 196C (entry requirements); s 520(1) (no civil penalty order after a conviction for the same conduct); s 523(1) table
-- (provisions open to an infringement notice); s 524(2), (4), (4A) (payment period and amount); s 532 (false or misleading
-- information); s 628 (forfeiture); s 635 (no privilege against self-incrimination; use immunity).
--
-- Edition: Biosecurity Act 2015, Compilation No. 21, compilation date 16 September 2026 ("Includes amendments: Act No. 84,
-- 2026"; register id C2026C00403, two volumes), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2015A00061.txt (retrieved
-- 2026-10-13).
-- Instruments and Acts used:
--   Regulatory Powers (Standard Provisions) Act 2014, Compilation No. 4, compilation date 20 March 2024, as deposited at
--   ../../../regulatory-powers-standard-provisions-act-2014/registers/source-bundle/C2014A00093.txt: s 82(5) (an
--   individual's civil penalty is at most the penalty specified), s 84(2) (one pecuniary penalty for the same conduct),
--   s 103(5) (a notice for conduct that is both an offence and a civil penalty contravention relates to the offence).
--   Crimes Act 1914 s 4AA (the penalty unit) is deposited, but its indexed figure is not, so a penalty unit is an INPUT.
--   NOT deposited, and therefore INPUTS: the Biosecurity (Prohibited and Conditionally Non-prohibited Goods)
--   Determinations, including the Conditionally Non-prohibited Goods Determination 2021 the plan names (whether a good is
--   prohibited, conditional or suspended, and its conditions); the Biosecurity Regulation 2016 (the "prescribed class of
--   persons" in s 196(1)(b), any shorter payment period under s 524(2)(b), any prescribed notice amount under s 524(4)(c));
--   the approved form of the incoming passenger declaration (which questions it asks); any s 196A determination.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: human biosecurity (Chapter 2); conveyances other than as the traveller's means of arrival (Chapter 4);
-- import permits (Part 3 Division 3) and permit conditions (s 187) beyond "a condition was not complied with"; ss 185(4),
-- (5) and 186(4), (5) (commercial advantage, environmental harm); s 188 (possession); biosecurity measures other than
-- destruction, and the review of a destruction decision (s 136(3)-(6)); Criminal Code ss 137.1, 137.2 (not deposited);
-- the Customs Act 1901 declaration on the same card (a separate duty, encoded in the Customs Act baseline); the Migration
-- Act (visa cancellation is not in this Act). Chloe's 2011 return is before this Act commenced (s 2): it is answered on
-- the 2026 text, and the Quarantine Act 1908 that then applied is not deposited.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the individual's seat with the design defaults ruled on
-- 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use as advice.
-- Judgments the law hands to an officer or the Directors are INPUTS ("if the biosecurity officer suspects, on reasonable
-- grounds, ..."). Money is in penalty units, or in dollars from a penalty-unit input.

§ `Biosecurity Act 2015`

§§ `s 2 -- did this Act apply when I arrived`

-- s 2(1) table, item 2: "Sections 3 to 645 ... A single day to be fixed by Proclamation. However, if the provisions do not
-- commence within the period of 12 months beginning on the day this Act receives the Royal Assent, they commence on the day
-- after the end of that period." Column 3: "16 June 2016".
@export Did the operative provisions of the Biosecurity Act 2015 apply on the day I arrived (s 2, commencement 16 June 2016)?
GIVEN `year of arrival` IS A NUMBER @desc The year you arrived in Australia
      `month of arrival` IS A NUMBER @desc The month you arrived (1 to 12)
      `day of arrival` IS A NUMBER @desc The day of the month you arrived
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Did the Act apply when I arrived` IF
        `year of arrival` > 2016
    OR  (`year of arrival` = 2016 AND `month of arrival` > 6)
    OR  (`year of arrival` = 2016 AND `month of arrival` = 6 AND `day of arrival` >= 16)

§§ `ss 119, 162 -- are my goods under biosecurity control`

-- s 119(1)-(2): goods "brought into Australian territory on an aircraft or vessel that has entered Australian territory
-- during a flight or voyage that commenced outside Australian territory ... become subject to biosecurity control when the
-- aircraft or vessel carrying the goods enters Australian territory." s 119(3): they remain so "until they are released
-- from biosecurity control under Division 10". s 162(1): released if a biosecurity officer notifies release, an approved
-- participant releases them, prescribed goods leave a release area, "the goods are destroyed" or "the goods leave
-- Australian territory".
DECLARE `My arrival` HAS
    `I came on a flight or voyage that began outside Australian territory` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you arrived on an international flight or voyage (one that began outside Australian territory)
    `I am in the prescribed class of persons` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you are in the class of persons the Biosecurity Regulation 2016 prescribes for s 196 (not deposited; for incoming passengers, ASSUMED yes)
    `the Director has required the declaration` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the Director of Biosecurity has required you to give information, for example on the incoming passenger declaration
    `the Director has required my passport` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you have been required to produce your passport or other travel document for biosecurity purposes
    `my goods have been released from biosecurity control` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether your goods have been released (a biosecurity officer told you so, they left a release area, were destroyed, or left Australia)
    `I have goods of a kind the declaration asks about` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you have goods (food, plant or animal material, soil, and so on) of a kind the approved form asks you to declare
    `I answered yes for those goods` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you answered yes to the declaration's questions about those goods
    `a s 196A entry requirement applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether an entry requirement determined by the Agriculture Minister under s 196A applies to you (not deposited)
    `I complied with the entry requirement` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you complied with that entry requirement

@export Are the goods I brought with me under biosecurity control (ss 119, 162)?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are my goods under biosecurity control` IF
        `my arrival`'s `I came on a flight or voyage that began outside Australian territory`
    AND (NOT `my arrival`'s `my goods have been released from biosecurity control`)

§§ `s 196 -- must I fill in the incoming passenger declaration`

-- s 196(1): "This section applies in relation to persons who: (a) intend to enter, or enter, Australian territory on an
-- incoming aircraft or vessel; and (b) are included in a prescribed class of persons."
-- s 196(2): "The Director of Biosecurity may require any person covered by subsection (1) ... to provide information
-- (including by answering questions) for the purpose of assessing the level of biosecurity risk associated with the person
-- and any goods that the person has with him or her." s 196(3): "in the manner approved by the Director".
-- s 196(4): "A person who is required to provide information under subsection (2) must comply with the requirement. Civil
-- penalty: 120 penalty units."
-- The duty depends on a prescribed class (not deposited) and on the Director's requirement: both are inputs.
@export Must I give the biosecurity information the incoming passenger declaration asks for (s 196(1), (2), (4))?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I fill in the biosecurity declaration` IF
        `my arrival`'s `I came on a flight or voyage that began outside Australian territory`
    AND `my arrival`'s `I am in the prescribed class of persons`
    AND `my arrival`'s `the Director has required the declaration`

-- s 196(3A)(a): the Director may "require any person covered by subsection (1) ... to produce a document covered by
-- subsection (3B)" -- "(a) the person's Australian travel document ...; (b) a passport, or other travel document, issued
-- ... by ... the government of a foreign country". s 196(5): must comply; civil penalty 120 penalty units.
@export Must I produce my passport for biosecurity purposes (s 196(3A), (3B), (5))?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I produce my passport for biosecurity` IF
        `my arrival`'s `I came on a flight or voyage that began outside Australian territory`
    AND `my arrival`'s `I am in the prescribed class of persons`
    AND `my arrival`'s `the Director has required my passport`

-- The questions themselves are in the form "approved by the Director" (s 196(3)), which is not deposited. Whether the
-- traveller has goods of a kind the form asks about is an input.
@export Do I have anything to declare for biosecurity on the incoming passenger declaration?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I have anything to declare for biosecurity` IF
        `Must I fill in the biosecurity declaration` `my arrival`
    AND `my arrival`'s `I have goods of a kind the declaration asks about`

@export Have I failed to declare goods the biosecurity declaration asks about?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I failed to declare my goods` IF
        `Do I have anything to declare for biosecurity` `my arrival`
    AND (NOT `my arrival`'s `I answered yes for those goods`)

-- s 196C: "An individual to whom a requirement determined under section 196A applies must comply with the requirement.
-- ... Civil penalty: 120 penalty units."
@export Am I in breach of an entry requirement determined under s 196A (s 196C)?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I in breach of an entry requirement` IF
        `my arrival`'s `a s 196A entry requirement applies to me`
    AND (NOT `my arrival`'s `I complied with the entry requirement`)

§§ `ss 126, 635 -- must I answer a biosecurity officer`

-- s 126(1): "A biosecurity officer may require a person who the biosecurity officer suspects, on reasonable grounds, has
-- information in relation to the goods to answer questions, or provide information in writing, in relation to the goods."
-- s 126(2): must comply; "Civil penalty: 300 penalty units."
@export Must I answer a biosecurity officer's questions about my goods (s 126)?
GIVEN `my goods are under biosecurity control` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods are under biosecurity control
      `the officer suspects on reasonable grounds that I have information about them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the biosecurity officer suspects, on reasonable grounds, that you have information about the goods
      `the officer has required me to answer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the officer has required you to answer questions or give information in writing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I answer the biosecurity officer` IF
        `my goods are under biosecurity control`
    AND `the officer suspects on reasonable grounds that I have information about them`
    AND `the officer has required me to answer`

-- s 635(1): "A person is not excused from answering a question, providing information or producing a document, under any of
-- the following provisions, on the ground that the answer ... might tend to incriminate the person ...: (b) sections 120,
-- 121, 122, 126 and 127 ...; (c) sections 193, 194, 195, 196, 196C, 200 and 201".
DECLARE `Information duty` IS ONE OF
    `the incoming passenger declaration (s 196)`
    `an entry requirement (s 196C)`
    `a biosecurity officer's questions about goods (s 126)`

@export May I refuse to answer because the answer might incriminate me (s 635(1))?
GIVEN `the duty` IS AN `Information duty` @desc Which provision requires the information
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I refuse to answer because it might incriminate me` IS
    CONSIDER `the duty`
    WHEN `the incoming passenger declaration (s 196)` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `an entry requirement (s 196C)` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a biosecurity officer's questions about goods (s 126)` THEN FALSE

-- s 635(2): for an individual, the answer and anything obtained from it "are not admissible in evidence against the
-- individual in any criminal or civil proceedings, except proceedings under, or arising out of, section 137.1 or 137.2 of
-- the Criminal Code or section 532 or 533 of this Act, (false or misleading information and documents) in relation to
-- answering the question".
@export Can what I said on the declaration, or to the officer, be used in evidence against me (s 635(2))?
GIVEN `the proceedings are for giving false or misleading information or documents in that answer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are under Criminal Code s 137.1 or 137.2 or s 532 or 533 of this Act, about that very answer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my answer be used against me` IF
    `the proceedings are for giving false or misleading information or documents in that answer`

§§ `ss 173, 174, 182 -- may my goods come in`

-- s 173(2): goods in a determination under s 173(1) "are prohibited goods". s 174(1)-(2): goods in a class the Directors
-- determine "must not be brought or imported into Australian territory unless specified conditions ... are complied with"
-- are "conditionally non-prohibited goods"; s 174(4)(a): a condition may be that a permit has been granted.
-- s 182(1)-(2): goods the Director determines "must not be brought or imported ... for a specified period" (at most 6
-- months) are "suspended goods"; s 182(7): the suspension "prevails" over an inconsistent s 174 determination.
-- The determinations are NOT deposited. Which determination catches a good is an INPUT.
DECLARE `My goods` HAS
    `they are in a prohibited goods determination` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the goods are specified, or in a class specified, in a determination under s 173 (not deposited)
    `they are in a suspended goods determination` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the goods are in a determination under s 182 suspending their import (not deposited)
    `they are in a conditionally non-prohibited goods determination` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the goods are in a class in a determination under s 174 (for example the 2021 Determination; not deposited)
    `every condition has been complied with` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether every condition the determination sets for the goods (including any permit) has been complied with
    `someone else failed the condition without my involvement` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the failure to meet the condition was someone else's act or omission, which you did not aid, procure or knowingly take part in (s 186(7))
    `they were concealed to stop an official finding them` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the goods were concealed (hidden, disguised, mislabelled or altered) to stop a biosecurity official finding them or their true nature (s 186A)

DECLARE `Import status` IS ONE OF
    `may come in: no determination catches them`
    `may come in: conditions complied with (s 174)`
    `may not come in: prohibited goods (s 173)`
    `may not come in: suspended goods (s 182)`
    `may not come in: a condition not complied with (s 174)`

@export May the goods I am bringing come into Australia (ss 173, 174, 182)?
GIVEN `the goods` IS A `My goods` @desc The facts about the goods
GIVETH AN `Import status`
DECIDE `May my goods come in` IS
    IF `the goods`'s `they are in a prohibited goods determination`
    THEN `may not come in: prohibited goods (s 173)`
    ELSE IF `the goods`'s `they are in a suspended goods determination`
         THEN `may not come in: suspended goods (s 182)`
         ELSE IF `the goods`'s `they are in a conditionally non-prohibited goods determination`
              THEN IF `the goods`'s `every condition has been complied with`
                   THEN `may come in: conditions complied with (s 174)`
                   ELSE `may not come in: a condition not complied with (s 174)`
              ELSE `may come in: no determination catches them`

§§ `ss 628, 131, 136 -- what can happen to my goods`

-- s 628(1): applies if goods brought in were, at that time, "(i) prohibited goods; or (ii) conditionally non-prohibited
-- goods; or (iii) suspended goods; and (c) if the goods were conditionally non-prohibited goods--an applicable condition in
-- relation to the goods has not been complied with." s 628(2): "A biosecurity officer may take possession of the goods."
-- s 628(3): "The Director of Biosecurity may determine that the goods are forfeited to the Commonwealth." s 628(4): before
-- that, the Director must notify the person in charge that the goods will be forfeited unless, within a specified period,
-- they arrange for the goods "to be destroyed, removed from Australian territory or otherwise dealt with"; s 628(6): a
-- failure to notify does not affect the forfeiture.
@export Can my goods be taken and forfeited to the Commonwealth (s 628)?
GIVEN `the goods` IS A `My goods` @desc The facts about the goods
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my goods be forfeited` IS
    CONSIDER `May my goods come in` `the goods`
    WHEN `may come in: no determination catches them` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `may come in: conditions complied with (s 174)` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 131: "If a biosecurity officer suspects, on reasonable grounds, that the level of biosecurity risk associated with goods
-- that are subject to biosecurity control is unacceptable, a biosecurity officer may require biosecurity measures to be
-- taken". s 136(1): "If a biosecurity officer considers, on reasonable grounds, that the goods cannot be effectively
-- treated, a biosecurity officer may ... require the goods to be destroyed." s 136(2): "must not require high-value goods to
-- be destroyed without the written approval of the Director of Biosecurity."
-- This applies to any goods under control, declared or not, prohibited or not.
@export Can my goods be destroyed for biosecurity reasons (ss 131, 136(1), (2))?
GIVEN `my goods are under biosecurity control` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods are under biosecurity control
      `the officer suspects on reasonable grounds the risk is unacceptable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a biosecurity officer suspects, on reasonable grounds, that the biosecurity risk of the goods is unacceptable
      `the officer considers on reasonable grounds they cannot be effectively treated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the officer considers, on reasonable grounds, that the goods cannot be effectively treated
      `they are high-value goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods are high-value goods
      `the Director has approved destruction in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Director of Biosecurity has given written approval to destroy them
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my goods be destroyed` IF
        `my goods are under biosecurity control`
    AND `the officer suspects on reasonable grounds the risk is unacceptable`
    AND `the officer considers on reasonable grounds they cannot be effectively treated`
    AND ((NOT `they are high-value goods`) OR `the Director has approved destruction in writing`)

§§ `ss 185, 186, 186A -- have I contravened the Act by bringing them in`

-- s 185(1): "A person contravenes this subsection if: (a) the person brings or imports goods into Australian territory; and
-- (b) the goods are: (i) prohibited goods; or (ii) suspended goods."
@export Have I contravened s 185(1) by bringing in prohibited or suspended goods?
GIVEN `the goods` IS A `My goods` @desc The facts about the goods
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I brought in prohibited or suspended goods` IF
        `the goods`'s `they are in a prohibited goods determination`
    OR  `the goods`'s `they are in a suspended goods determination`

-- s 186(1): contravened if the goods "are conditionally non-prohibited goods; and (c) a condition ... has not been complied
-- with." s 186(7): does not apply if the person "(a) did not do the act, or omit to do the act, that constituted the failure
-- ...; and (b) did not aid, abet, counsel or procure that act or omission; and (c) was not in any way knowingly concerned
-- in, or party to, that act or omission". The defendant bears an evidential burden.
-- Read with s 182(7): suspended goods are dealt with under s 185, not here.
@export Have I contravened s 186(1) by bringing in conditional goods without meeting a condition?
GIVEN `the goods` IS A `My goods` @desc The facts about the goods
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I breached a condition on conditional goods` IF
        `May my goods come in` `the goods` EQUALS `may not come in: a condition not complied with (s 174)`
    AND (NOT `the goods`'s `someone else failed the condition without my involvement`)

-- s 186A(1): a civil penalty of 1,200 penalty units if, in addition, "the goods are concealed for the purpose of preventing
-- the goods from being found, or preventing the true nature of the goods from being determined, by a biosecurity
-- official"; s 186A(3): the same exception as s 186(7).
@export Am I liable for concealing conditional goods (s 186A)?
GIVEN `the goods` IS A `My goods` @desc The facts about the goods
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I liable for concealing my goods` IF
        `Have I breached a condition on conditional goods` `the goods`
    AND `the goods`'s `they were concealed to stop an official finding them`

§§ `s 532 -- is my false answer a contravention`

-- s 532(1): "A person is liable to a civil penalty if: (a) the person gives information in compliance or purported compliance
-- with this Act; and (b) the person does so knowing that the information: (i) is false or misleading; or (ii) omits any
-- matter or thing without which the information is misleading. Civil penalty: 600 penalty units."
-- s 532(2), (3): not if the information is not false or misleading "in a material particular".
-- s 532(4): not if "the official did not take reasonable steps to inform the person that the person may be liable to a civil
-- penalty"; s 532(5): "it is sufficient if the following form of words is used: 'You may be liable to a civil penalty for
-- giving false or misleading information'".
DECLARE `My answer` HAS
    `given in compliance with the Act` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the answer was given in compliance or purported compliance with the Act (the declaration under s 196, or answers under s 126)
    `false, misleading or leaving something out` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the answer was false or misleading, or left out something without which it was misleading
    `in a material particular` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether it was false or misleading in a material particular (something that matters)
    `I knew it` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you knew the answer was false or misleading, or knew it left something out
    `I was warned I may be liable to a civil penalty` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the official took reasonable steps to tell you that you may be liable to a civil penalty (for example the warning printed on the declaration)

@export Am I liable to a civil penalty for a false or misleading answer (s 532)?
GIVEN `the answer` IS A `My answer` @desc The facts about what you wrote or said
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I liable for a false answer` IF
        `the answer`'s `given in compliance with the Act`
    AND `the answer`'s `false, misleading or leaving something out`
    AND `the answer`'s `in a material particular`
    AND `the answer`'s `I knew it`
    AND `the answer`'s `I was warned I may be liable to a civil penalty`

§§ `ss 185, 186, 186A, 196, 196C, 126, 532 -- what is the most I can be made to pay`

-- The penalties, as the text states them for an individual. For civil penalties, Regulatory Powers Act s 82(5)(b): the
-- penalty "must not be more than ... the pecuniary penalty specified for the civil penalty provision" (body corporate: 5
-- times).
DECLARE `Contravention` IS ONE OF
    `bringing in prohibited or suspended goods, fault-based offence (s 185(2))`
    `bringing in prohibited or suspended goods, strict liability offence (s 185(2A))`
    `bringing in prohibited or suspended goods, civil penalty (s 185(3))`
    `breaching a condition on conditional goods, fault-based offence (s 186(2))`
    `breaching a condition on conditional goods, civil penalty (s 186(3))`
    `concealing conditional goods, civil penalty (s 186A(1))`
    `not giving the declaration information, civil penalty (s 196(4))`
    `not producing my passport, civil penalty (s 196(5))`
    `breaching an entry requirement, civil penalty (s 196C)`
    `not answering a biosecurity officer, civil penalty (s 126(2))`
    `a false or misleading answer, civil penalty (s 532(1))`

-- s 185(2): "Imprisonment for 5 years or 1,000 penalty units, or both." s 185(2A): "60 penalty units." s 185(3): "1,000
-- penalty units." s 186(2): "Imprisonment for 5 years or 1,000 penalty units, or both." s 186(3): "1,000 penalty units."
-- s 186A(1): "1,200 penalty units." s 196(4), (5), s 196C: "120 penalty units." s 126(2): "300 penalty units." s 532(1):
-- "600 penalty units."
@export What is the most a court can make me pay for this contravention, in penalty units?
GIVEN `the contravention` IS A `Contravention` @desc The contravention alleged
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be made to pay, in penalty units` IS
    CONSIDER `the contravention`
    WHEN `bringing in prohibited or suspended goods, fault-based offence (s 185(2))` THEN 1000
    WHEN `bringing in prohibited or suspended goods, strict liability offence (s 185(2A))` THEN 60
    WHEN `bringing in prohibited or suspended goods, civil penalty (s 185(3))` THEN 1000
    WHEN `breaching a condition on conditional goods, fault-based offence (s 186(2))` THEN 1000
    WHEN `breaching a condition on conditional goods, civil penalty (s 186(3))` THEN 1000
    WHEN `concealing conditional goods, civil penalty (s 186A(1))` THEN 1200
    WHEN `not giving the declaration information, civil penalty (s 196(4))` THEN 120
    WHEN `not producing my passport, civil penalty (s 196(5))` THEN 120
    WHEN `breaching an entry requirement, civil penalty (s 196C)` THEN 120
    WHEN `not answering a biosecurity officer, civil penalty (s 126(2))` THEN 300
    WHEN `a false or misleading answer, civil penalty (s 532(1))` THEN 600

@export What is the longest I can be imprisoned for this contravention, in years?
GIVEN `the contravention` IS A `Contravention` @desc The contravention alleged
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the longest I can be imprisoned, in years` IS
    CONSIDER `the contravention`
    WHEN `bringing in prohibited or suspended goods, fault-based offence (s 185(2))` THEN 5
    WHEN `breaching a condition on conditional goods, fault-based offence (s 186(2))` THEN 5
    OTHERWISE 0

@export What is the most a court can make me pay for this contravention, in dollars?
GIVEN `the contravention` IS A `Contravention` @desc The contravention alleged
      `a penalty unit in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of a penalty unit (Crimes Act s 4AA: 330 dollars before indexation)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be made to pay, in dollars` IS
    `What is the most I can be made to pay, in penalty units` `the contravention` * `a penalty unit in dollars`

§§ `ss 523, 524 -- can I be given an infringement notice, and for how much`

-- s 523(1) table: the provisions "subject to an infringement notice" include item 3 "Subsection 126(2)", item 8AB
-- "Subsection 185(2A)", item 8A "Subsection 186A(1)", item 10 "Subsection 196(4)", item 10AA "Subsection 196(5)", item 10A
-- "Section 196C", item 43 "Subsection 532(1)". Not listed: s 185(2), 185(3), 186(2), 186(3).
-- Regulatory Powers Act s 103(5): if the conduct would be both a civil penalty contravention and an offence, "the
-- infringement notice must relate to the alleged contravention of the offence provision" -- so a notice for prohibited goods
-- is for the s 185(2A) strict liability offence.
@export Can I be given an infringement notice for this contravention (s 523(1))?
GIVEN `the contravention` IS A `Contravention` @desc The contravention alleged
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be given an infringement notice` IS
    CONSIDER `the contravention`
    WHEN `bringing in prohibited or suspended goods, strict liability offence (s 185(2A))` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `concealing conditional goods, civil penalty (s 186A(1))` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `not giving the declaration information, civil penalty (s 196(4))` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `not producing my passport, civil penalty (s 196(5))` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `breaching an entry requirement, civil penalty (s 196C)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `not answering a biosecurity officer, civil penalty (s 126(2))` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a false or misleading answer, civil penalty (s 532(1))` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 524(4): the amount "must be the least of: (a) one-fifth of the maximum penalty that a court could impose on the person
-- for that contravention; and (b) 12 penalty units where the person is an individual ...; and (c) if the regulations
-- prescribe a different number of penalty units for the alleged contravention of the provision by the person--that number
-- of penalty units." s 524(4A): "Despite subsection (4)", for s 186A(1), "(a) 20 penalty units where the person is an
-- individual".
-- READING: on these words a prescribed number counts only if it is the least, so regulations can lower the amount but not
-- raise it. The Biosecurity Regulation 2016 is not deposited; any prescribed number is an input (0 = none prescribed).
@export How much would an infringement notice ask me to pay, in penalty units (s 524(4), (4A))?
GIVEN `the contravention` IS A `Contravention` @desc The contravention alleged
      `penalty units prescribed by the regulations` IS A NUMBER @desc Any number of penalty units the regulations prescribe for this contravention (0 if none; not deposited)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much would an infringement notice ask me to pay, in penalty units` IS
    IF `the contravention` EQUALS `concealing conditional goods, civil penalty (s 186A(1))`
    THEN 20
    ELSE IF `penalty units prescribed by the regulations` > 0 AND `penalty units prescribed by the regulations` < `the statutory notice amount` `the contravention`
         THEN `penalty units prescribed by the regulations`
         ELSE `the statutory notice amount` `the contravention`
    WHERE
        `the statutory notice amount` `c` MEANS
            IF `What is the most I can be made to pay, in penalty units` c / 5 < 12
            THEN `What is the most I can be made to pay, in penalty units` c / 5
            ELSE 12

-- s 524(2): the period must be "(a) 28 days after the day the notice is given; or (b) if the regulations prescribe a period
-- that ends earlier--the prescribed period." Note: it "may ... even" end "on the day that the notice is given".
@export Within how many days of being given an infringement notice must I pay it (s 524(2))?
GIVEN `the regulations prescribe an earlier period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulations prescribe a shorter period for this kind of contravention (not deposited)
      `days in the prescribed period` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days in that prescribed period (0 = the same day)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `Within how many days must I pay the infringement notice` IS
    IF `the regulations prescribe an earlier period` AND `days in the prescribed period` < 28
    THEN `days in the prescribed period`
    ELSE 28

§§ `s 520(1); Regulatory Powers Act s 84(2) -- the customs conviction and the biosecurity penalty`

-- s 520(1): "A relevant court may not make a civil penalty order ... for a contravention ... of a civil penalty provision of
-- this Act if: (a) the person has been convicted of an offence under an Australian law; or (b) the person has been found by
-- a court to have contravened a civil penalty provision under an Australian law; that is constituted by conduct that is the
-- same, or substantially the same, as the conduct constituting the biosecurity contravention."
-- So a conviction under the Customs Act 1901 (for example s 233(1)(b), importing prohibited imports) for the same conduct
-- bars a biosecurity civil penalty order. Whether the conduct is "substantially the same" is for the court: an input.
@export Can a court still order me to pay a biosecurity civil penalty after I was convicted, or found liable, for the same conduct under another Australian law (s 520(1))?
GIVEN `I was convicted or found liable under an Australian law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been convicted of an offence, or found to have contravened a civil penalty provision, under an Australian law (for example the Customs Act 1901)
      `the court finds it the same or substantially the same conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that conviction or finding was for conduct the same, or substantially the same, as the biosecurity contravention
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I still be ordered to pay a biosecurity civil penalty` IF
    NOT (`I was convicted or found liable under an Australian law` AND `the court finds it the same or substantially the same conduct`)

§§ `visas -- does this Act say my visa can be cancelled`

-- The word "visa" does not occur in the deposited compilation (C2026C00403). Nothing in this Act provides for a visa to be
-- cancelled for a biosecurity contravention; any such consequence would come from the Migration Act 1958, which is not
-- encoded here. This decision records what the text states, not what another Act may do.
@export Does the Biosecurity Act 2015 itself provide for my visa to be cancelled for not declaring goods or for a false answer?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does this Act provide for my visa to be cancelled` IS FALSE
